  "question": {
    "text": "Consider two polaroids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> as shown. Unpolarised light is incident on polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>. Now, both the polaroids are rotated simultaneously by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>180</mn><mo>°</mo></math> in the same sense of rotation such that at every instant, their pass (transmission) axes always remain parallel to each other. During the rotation, intensity of transmitted light through polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math><br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0092_055f9d613484.png\" style=\"width: 191px; height: 136px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "increases continuously",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "first increases then decreases",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "remains same",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "decreases continuously",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Intensity after passing through polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> is<br /><br /><math><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></math><br /><br /><math><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi></math><br /><br />Intensity after passing through polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is<br /><br /><math><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></math><br /><br />Here, <math><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo></math> angle between pass axes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>.<br /><br />Here, pass (transmission) axes always remain parallel to each other i.e. <math><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> .<br /><br /><math><mo>∴</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></math><br /><br />Hence, during the rotation, the intensity of transmitted light through polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> remains the same."
  }
